Rebuilds the terrain mesh, restricted to a new azimuth wedge

SYNOPSIS

    import horizonator

    h = horizonator.horizonator(45.77294, 4.82993, 400, 200,
                                restrict_mesh_azimuth = True,
                                mesh_az_deg0 = -15, mesh_az_deg1 = 15)

    for az0 in range(-180, 180, 30):
        h.rebuild_mesh(az0, az0+30)
        image, ranges = h.render(az0, az0+30)
        ...

DESCRIPTION

Lets a caller render a wide panorama as a sequence of narrow tiles, each
with a memory-bounded mesh, all on the SAME already-inited context: this
function replaces the mesh built by the constructor (or by a previous
call to this function) with one restricted to [mesh_az_deg0,mesh_az_deg1]
instead, WITHOUT recreating the underlying GL context.

This distinction matters: repeatedly constructing a new horizonator
object (one context per tile) leaks memory inside the Mesa/llvmpipe
software-rendering driver itself -- confirmed empirically, and outside
this project's code to fix. Building one object and calling
rebuild_mesh() between tiles avoids that leak entirely (verified flat
memory across many tiles in a row).

The constructor's own restrict_mesh_azimuth/mesh_az_deg0/mesh_az_deg1
arguments only set the INITIAL wedge (or, if restrict_mesh_azimuth is
left at its default of False, mesh the whole loaded circle instead, with
no bound on the mesh's memory footprint -- fine for a single moderate
render, risky for a wide panorama at high resolution, e.g. with
SRTM1=True). Call this function to change the wedge afterwards.

ARGUMENTS

- mesh_az_deg0, mesh_az_deg1: the new azimuth wedge to mesh, in degrees.
  Should comfortably cover the az_deg0,az_deg1 that the following
  render(...) call(s) will use -- a small margin is added internally, but
  looking far outside [mesh_az_deg0,mesh_az_deg1] will leave gaps

RETURNED VALUES

None. Raises an exception on failure.
